"""Add WGSL chunks to the engine's built-in .shaderpkg set for a web export.

The engine's built-in passes (tonemap, the depth compute chain, clustered light
culling, the shadow and sky packages) ship as .shaderpkg files holding SPIR-V
plus reflection metadata. A browser ingests no SPIR-V, so for each package this
cooks the same GLSL stage sources through Tools/ShaderCook
(glslc vulkan1.1 -> spirv-opt split -> naga -> tint) and writes a copy with a
"<stage>-wgsl" chunk appended. Reflection metadata is generated by the engine's host ShaderReflect tool from
the same compatibility-profile GLSL, before push-constant lowering, and the same
tool writes and reads every package: nothing here parses the container. Native and
compatibility shaders can declare different storage formats and binding sets;
their packages must carry the matching metadata.

Not every built-in ships as a package. The UI SDF renderer and the editor's
overlays load bare stage bytecode through Utils::LoadShaderFile, building their
descriptor layouts in C++ rather than from reflection metadata; those cook to
plain .wgsl files beside the packages, and the emscripten arm of
LoadShaderFile prefers the .wgsl sibling of the .spv name it was asked for.

Material variants are a different path again: those come from
MaterialVariantCook, which composes per-material source. This module owns only
the fixed built-in set, whose stage sources are listed below because nothing at
runtime knows the mapping from package name back to .vert/.frag/.comp.

    python3 Tools/Web/builtin_shader_cook.py --editor \\
        --shaderpkg-dir build/macos-arm64-ninja/Shaders --out /tmp/webeditor-shaders
"""

from __future__ import annotations

